WEBVTT

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:17.610
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's a special Christmas edition of This American Life . We have a bunch of things for you today, including later in the program, "Christmas on Fire." But let's just kick things off right now with a song recorded at an event we did onstage a few weeks ago.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，《美国生活》圣诞特别版节目。今天我们要为大家呈现许多精彩内容，包括节目后半段的“圣诞着火了”。但让我们先从几周前我们在舞台上录制的一首歌开始吧。

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:49.370
<v Woman>(SINGING) On the 12th day of Christmas, my true love gave to me 12 drummers drumming, 11 pipers piping, 10 lords a leaping, nine ladies dancing, eight maids a milking, seven swans a swimming, six geese a laying, five golden rings, four calling birds, three French hens, two turtle doves, and a partridge in a pear tree.
<v Woman>（歌唱）圣诞节第12天，我的真爱送我：12个击鼓的鼓手，11个吹笛的风笛手，10个跳跃的领主，9个跳舞的女士，8个挤奶的女佣，7个游泳的天鹅，6个下蛋的鹅，5个金戒指，4个鸣叫的鸟，3个法国母鸡，2个斑鸠，以及梨树上的一只鹧鸪。

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:51.350
<v Ira Glass>I have always hated that song.
<v Ira Glass>我一直很讨厌这首歌。

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:53.070
<v Ira Glass>[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>[观众笑声]

00:00:53.070 --> 00:01:04.680
<v Ira Glass>I have always hated that song. I feel like that song is symbolic of everything that is bad about Christmas. I should say I have no bad feelings about Christmas. I am a noncombatant when it comes to Christmas.
<v Ira Glass>我一直很讨厌这首歌。我觉得这首歌象征着圣诞节所有糟糕的东西。我得说，我对圣诞节没什么恶感。说到圣诞节，我算是个非战斗人员。

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:20.150
<v Woman>I was onstage talking about this song because we had this idea that we thought might make a really fun thing to try for Christmas. And that is we organized a night of improv comedy. And I know maybe you're saying, OK, how does that go together? Why improv comedy and Christmas?
<v Woman>我在舞台上谈论这首歌，是因为我们有个想法，觉得这可能是圣诞节尝试的一件非常有趣的事情。我们组织了一场即兴喜剧之夜。我知道，你可能会说，好的，这有什么关联？为什么是即兴喜剧和圣诞节？

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:49.930
<v Woman>OK, well, this is going to take a little explaining. Stay with me. This British writer and performer named Daniel Kitson-- I saw him say this thing about Christmas that seems so true. He says that when you're a kid and you wake up Christmas morning, what makes Christmas feel so magical is this feeling of, like, "Oh, my god. It's going to be amazing. The world is so full of possibility. Anything can happen." The fact that Santa Claus even exists, this literally magical being who came to your home.
<v Woman>好吧，这需要一点解释。听我慢慢道来。这位英国作家兼表演者丹尼尔·基特森（Daniel Kitson）——我曾看他讲过一件事，关于圣诞节的，似乎非常有道理。他说，当你还是个孩子，圣诞节早上醒来，让圣诞节感觉如此神奇的，是这种感觉，就像，“天哪，这太棒了。世界充满了无限可能。任何事情都可能发生。”圣诞老人的存在，这个实际上神奇的存在，来到你家，这一事实。

00:01:49.930 --> 00:02:19.670
<v Woman>And Kitson says this thrilled feeling that anything can happen, that is the thing that is so hard to hold onto once we become adults. And in fact, he says that so many adults at Christmas, they try to recreate the same Christmas year after year with the same everything over and over and over. It's like they lock in. Christmas becomes the opposite of "anything can happen." That is why it never feels as magical to adults. But it is possible to get back to that feeling.
<v Woman>基特森说，这种觉得什么都可能发生的激动感，是我们成年后最难保持的东西。事实上，他说很多成年人在圣诞节时，试图年复一年地重现同一个圣诞节，重复着同样的一切。这就像他们被锁定住了。圣诞节变成了“任何事情都可能发生”的反面。这就是为什么成年人从不觉得它那么神奇。但要找回那种感觉是有可能的。

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:45.420
<v Woman>And, OK, back to the plan for today's show. One place that adults embrace that feeling that anything could happen and it's going to happen and then they make it happen is improv comedy, where, you know, they walk on stage with no idea what anybody is going to say. And then one person tries something. They say something. And then somebody else pitches in with a line of their own, and then somebody else pitches in. And before you know it, they've made like a whole scene, a whole world, really.
<v Woman>好了，回到今天节目的计划。成年人拥抱那种“任何事情都可能发生，而且即将发生，然后他们让它发生”的感觉的地方，就是即兴喜剧。他们走上舞台，完全不知道别人会说什么。然后一个人尝试做点什么，说点什么。接着另一个人加入一句自己的台词，然后又有人加入。在你意识到之前，他们已经创造出了一整个场景，一整个世界。

00:02:45.420 --> 00:03:11.080
<v Woman>And so with that in mind, we got these incredible improv comedians, and we got a club to perform in, and we did a show. And we did the show in this particular style of improv, where the people on stage, they tell true stories about their lives. And then those stories, those true stories, become the basis of the improv scenes that they then invent. And those scenes lead them to other true stories that then lead them into other improv scenes and so on and so on and so on.
<v Woman>考虑到这一点，我们请来了这些出色的即兴喜剧演员，找了一个演出俱乐部，做了一场秀。我们以这种特殊的即兴风格表演，舞台上的人讲述他们生活中的真实故事。然后这些故事，这些真实的故事，成为他们随后发明的即兴表演场景的基础。而那些场景引导他们走向其他真实故事，进而带入更多的即兴场景，如此循环往复。

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:24.680
<v Woman>And I was there. I kicked things off with a story about that song, about the song "The 12 Days of Christmas," which is a song that I noticed, even though-- just to say it again-- Christmas is not my day.
<v Woman>当时我在现场。我先讲了一个关于那首歌的故事，关于《圣诞节的12天》这首歌。我注意到，尽管——再次声明——圣诞节不是我的节日。

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:38.590
<v Ira Glass>I am not somebody who celebrates Christmas at all. I'm a Jew. And I'm not that kind of Jew whose parents celebrated Christmas on Hanukkah. We were pure Hanukkah people, and we look down on the Christmas Hanukkah Jews.
<v Ira Glass>我根本不是一个庆祝圣诞节的人。我是犹太人。我也不是那种父母会过光明节同时过圣诞节的犹太人。我们是纯正的光明节人士，我们瞧不起那些过圣诞节的光明节犹太人。

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:41.350
<v Ira Glass>[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>[观众笑声]

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:56.540
<v Ira Glass>And in fact, I grew up in a suburb of Baltimore that was so Jewish, I didn't understand that we were in the minority. And so Christmas, I do nothing. It means nothing to me. The day means nothing. And most years, I go to Vegas on Christmas.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，我在巴尔的摩郊区长大，那里犹太人非常多，我甚至没意识到我们是少数派。所以圣诞节，我什么都不做。它对我来说毫无意义。这一天什么也不是。大多数年份，我在圣诞节时会去拉斯维加斯。

00:03:56.540 --> 00:04:40.450
<v Ira Glass>But I do really love Christmas music. I've always loved Christmas music. And at some point in my 20s, I started to make a collection of Christmas CDs and records. And the worst song of all is the song "The 12 Days of Christmas." And whenever it comes on, I just think, like, why is this still in circulation? First of all, the words refer to a wife and a world that none of us can relate to or care about. "12 lords a leaping," like, what is-- what experience is that even referring to? And then swans are swimming, geese are laying. So are we in a warm climate, a cold climate? It just raises so many questions.
<v Ira Glass>但我真的很喜欢圣诞音乐。我一直很喜欢圣诞音乐。在我20多岁的时候，我开始收集圣诞CD和唱片。所有歌曲中最糟糕的就是《圣诞节的12天》。每当它响起，我就想，为什么这首歌还在流传？首先，歌词指的是一个我们谁都无法共鸣或不在乎的妻子和一个世界。“10个跳跃的领主”，这到底指的是什么体验？还有游泳的天鹅，下蛋的鹅。所以我们是在温暖的气候还是寒冷的气候？这引发了太多问题。

00:04:40.450 --> 00:05:10.780
<v Ira Glass>But also the form of the song is so intensely boring. And you guys know what I'm talking about, where you do the first one, and then you go back and you do all the ones before it, which you already did. You know I mean? So you do two turtle doves and a partridge in a pear tree. Then three French hens, two turtle doves, and a partridge in a pear tree. Then four calling birds, three French hens, two turtle doves and a partridge in a pear tree. Five golden rings, four calling birds. And so like-- and so you know what's going to happen. It's repetitious in the most boring way, and then it's always sung very slowly, which is annoying.
<v Ira Glass>而且这首歌的形式极其枯燥。你们知道我在说什么，先唱第一句，然后倒回去唱之前的所有歌词，而那些你已经唱过了。你懂我的意思吗？所以你唱2个斑鸠和1只梨树上的鹧鸪。然后是3个法国母鸡，2个斑鸠，1只梨树上的鹧鸪。然后是4个鸣叫的鸟，3个法国母鸡，2个斑鸠，1只梨树上的鹧鸪。5个金戒指，4个鸣叫的鸟。所以……你知道会发生什么。它以最无聊的方式重复，而且总是唱得非常慢，这很烦人。

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:36.790
<v Ira Glass>And I don't know why it survived hundred of years to still be around. And my only thought is "five golden rings." "Five golden rings" is the hook of the song. And you've sung the song, right? When you get to five golden rings, everybody breathes a sigh of relief. It's like, (SINGING) five golden rings. And you just feel like, yeah!
<v Ira Glass>我不知道为什么它能存活几百年还存在着。我唯一的想法就是“5个金戒指”。“5个金戒指”是这首歌的钩子（洗脑点）。你们唱过这首歌，对吧？当唱到“5个金戒指”时，每个人都会松一口气。就像，（歌唱）5个金戒指。你只觉得，耶！

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:46.010
<v Ira Glass>You know what I mean? It's like the drum solo. That's the hook of the song. And without five golden rings, this song would be nothing.
<v Ira Glass>你懂我的意思吗？这就像鼓手独奏。那是这首歌的钩子。没有那5个金戒指，这首歌将一文不值。

00:05:46.010 --> 00:06:18.130
<v Woman>Okay, so at this point-- this is me back in the studio talking-- at this point, if I could just describe the stage. Sitting on stools in a row, there are six improv comedians. And we got some of the best people around for this kind of thing. Seriously, we felt so lucky they agreed to be in the show. Aidy Bryant and Sasheer Zamata, they're both on Saturday Night Live . Scott Adsit, he was on 30 Rock , and before that, he was in Second City. So was Tammy Sagher. Chris Gethard and Mike Birbiglia, they've both been on our radio program before, Mike many, many times. They both have deep improv backgrounds.
<v Woman>好的，现在——这是我在录音室里说话——此时，如果我能描述一下舞台。六个即兴喜剧演员并排坐在凳子上。我们为这种表演找来了一些最棒的人选。说真的，我们感到非常幸运他们同意参加演出。艾迪·布莱恩特（Aidy Bryant）和萨希尔·扎马塔（Sasheer Zamata），她们都是《周六夜现场》的成员。斯科特·阿西特（Scott Adsit），他曾出演《我为喜剧狂》，在此之前，他是第二城剧团的成员。塔米·萨格（Tammy Sagher）也是如此。克里斯·格哈德（Chris Gethard）和迈克·比比利亚（Mike Birbiglia），他们之前都上过我们的广播节目，迈克来过很多很多次。他们都有深厚的即兴表演背景。

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:26.770
<v Woman>So six of them are sitting on the stage on these stools. And they listen to that first story that I told to kick things off. And Mike was the first one to stand up and speak.
<v Woman>所以他们六个人坐在舞台上的凳子上。他们听着我开场讲的第一个故事。迈克是第一个站起来发言的人。

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:48.470
<v Mike Birbiglia>Wow. Well, first of all I want to disagree with Ira about that song. I think in some sense, it's sort of the "15 Bottles of Beer on the Wall" of Christmas carols. It's anthemic. It's repetitive. I think where the disconnect happens is that it's just British things.
<v Mike Birbiglia>哇。首先，我想就那首歌和艾拉（Ira）持不同意见。我觉得某种意义上，它就像圣诞颂歌版的《墙上的15瓶啤酒》。它极具煽动性，重复性强。我觉得断层发生的原因是它全是英国玩意儿。

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:54.500
<v Mike Birbiglia>[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]
<v Mike Birbiglia>[观众笑声]

00:06:54.500 --> 00:07:01.870
<v Woman>What kind of American things would you put in there? Like a bald eagle? A bald eagle in a-- in a what, oak tree?
<v Woman>你会放些什么样的美国玩意儿进去？比如秃鹰？秃鹰在——在什么树上，橡树吗？

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:09.190
<v Mike Birbiglia>Yeah. Bald eagle in an oak tree. Yeah, we just got to de-British the whole thing. I think that's the problem.
<v Mike Birbiglia>对。橡树上的秃鹰。没错，我们得把这整首歌“去英国化”。我觉得那是关键问题所在。

00:07:09.190 --> 00:07:11.360
<v Mike Birbiglia>Lady Haversquire.
<v Mike Birbiglia>海弗斯夸尔夫人（Lady Haversquire）。

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:13.210
<v Lady Haversquire>Yes?
<v Lady Haversquire>什么事？

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:18.140
<v Mike Birbiglia>We've been courting now for going on 10 months. And--
<v Mike Birbiglia>我们求爱已经快10个月了。而且——

00:07:18.140 --> 00:07:20.270
<v Lady Haversquire>Yes?
<v Lady Haversquire>是吗？

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:24.880
<v Mike Birbiglia>--I've been playing in my mind with what to get you for Christmas.
<v Mike Birbiglia>——我一直在心里盘算圣诞节给你买什么。

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:39.240
<v Woman>Lord Mavermunch, I am sure that whatever you will bring me on Christmas will bring me much-- what?
<v Woman>马弗蒙奇勋爵（Lord Mavermunch），我相信无论你圣诞节带给我什么，都会带给我……什么？

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:41.960
<v Lord Mavermunch>May I give it you now?
<v Lord Mavermunch>我可以现在就给你吗？

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:45.790
<v Woman>Oh! Yes, but so soon before Christmas?
<v Woman>噢！可以，但距离圣诞节还有这么久？

00:07:45.790 --> 00:07:50.550
<v Lord Mavermunch>I'm excited. I think I did well.
<v Lord Mavermunch>我很兴奋。我觉得我表现得很好。

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:51.980
<v Woman>All right.
<v Woman>好吧。

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:57.030
<v Lord Mavermunch>Here you are-- five golden rings.
<v Lord Mavermunch>给你——5个金戒指。

00:07:57.030 --> 00:08:00.060
<v Woman>Oh. Oh my goodness.
<v Woman>噢。天哪。

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:06.010
<v Lord Mavermunch>But I've also brought you 23 birds.
<v Lord Mavermunch>但我还给你带了23只鸟。

00:08:06.010 --> 00:08:07.780
<v Woman>Sorry?
<v Woman>抱歉？

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:14.010
<v Lord Mavermunch>23 individual birds and 50 human people.
<v Lord Mavermunch>23只独立的鸟，还有50个人类。

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:18.770
<v Lord Mavermunch>[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]
<v Lord Mavermunch>[观众笑声]

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:21.170
<v Lord Mavermunch>Could you stay in a group, please?
<v Lord Mavermunch>你们能不能聚在一起？

00:08:21.170 --> 00:08:22.670
<v Lord Mavermunch>[GROUP TALKING OVER EACH OTHER]
<v Lord Mavermunch>[人群嘈杂声]

00:08:22.670 --> 00:08:24.570
<v Woman>Guys, guys, guys.
<v Woman>伙计们，伙计们。

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:28.360
<v Man>We are the Christmas presents.
<v Man>我们是圣诞礼物。

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:32.420
<v Lord Mavermunch>Do you like it?
<v Lord Mavermunch>你喜欢吗？

00:08:32.420 --> 00:08:40.950
<v Lady Haversquire>I-- I love it. Um, Lord Mavermunch, um, if we could just go back for a moment to the five golden rings.
<v Lady Haversquire>我——我很喜欢。嗯，马弗蒙奇勋爵，嗯，如果我们能回到刚才的5个金戒指……

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:43.340
<v Lord Mavermunch>Ah, yes.
<v Lord Mavermunch>啊，是的。

00:08:43.340 --> 00:09:03.910
<v Lady Haversquire>Yes. Ah, so two questions. Question number one-- what is the difference between a gold ring and a golden ring? Because one seems to imply that it merely has the color, yet none of the value.
<v Lady Haversquire>是的。啊，所以有两个问题。第一个问题——金戒指（gold ring）和镀金戒指（golden ring）有什么区别？因为一个似乎暗示它仅仅拥有颜色，却没有任何价值。

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:17.730
<v Lord Mavermunch>Well, you've caught me there. I'm afraid to say the pear tree cost rather more than I expected.
<v Lord Mavermunch>好吧，你问住我了。恐怕梨树的成本比我预期的要高。

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:20.200
<v Lady Haversquire>Lord Mavermunch?
<v Lady Haversquire>马弗蒙奇勋爵？

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:21.830
<v Lord Mavermunch>Lady Haversquire?
<v Lord Mavermunch>海弗斯夸尔夫人？

00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:25.410
<v Lady Haversquire>Do you remember when you first came courting?
<v Lady Haversquire>你记得你刚开始追求我的时候吗？

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:28.760
<v Lord Mavermunch>Oh, yes. My memory is intact.
<v Lord Mavermunch>噢，当然。我的记忆力很完好。

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:44.800
<v Lady Haversquire>Was it? Because I distinctly remember telling you I don't like a turtle dove. I find roses played out. And quite frankly, lords a leaping I've seen enough. And yet that's all you seem to have brought.
<v Lady Haversquire>是吗？因为我清楚地记得告诉你我不喜欢斑鸠。我觉得玫瑰花太俗气了。说实话，跳跃的领主我已经看够了。然而你似乎只带了这些。

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:45.950
<v Lord 1>Oh, come on!
<v Lord 1>噢，拜托！

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:47.360
<v Lord 2>Oh, hey, come on!
<v Lord 2>噢，嘿，拜托！

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:50.860
<v Lord 1>We're so good! Watch this! Hut, hut, hut!
<v Lord 1>我们很棒！看着！嘿，嘿，嘿！

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:55.390
<v Lord 2>[GRUNTING]
<v Lord 2>[呻吟声]

00:09:55.390 --> 00:09:58.580
<v Lord 1>I'm going so high!
<v Lord 1>我跳得好高！

00:09:58.580 --> 00:10:01.240
<v Lord Mavermunch>Look, just--
<v Lord Mavermunch>看，只是——

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:05.610
<v Lady Haversquire>Do you know what they call you in the county?
<v Lady Haversquire>你知道郡里的人怎么称呼你吗？

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:07.570
<v Lord Mavermunch>I don't.
<v Lord Mavermunch>我不知道。

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:10.840
<v Lady Haversquire>They call you Quantity over Quality.
<v Lady Haversquire>他们叫你“追求数量不顾质量”。

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:12.300
<v Woman>Scene.
<v Woman>场景结束。

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:21.050
<v Woman>[AUDIENCE CHEERING]
<v Woman>[观众欢呼]

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:27.220
<v Ira Glass>I wonder if you guys actually have memories-- did any of you have to go caroling? Or did you sing in--
<v Ira Glass>我好奇你们有没有真实的记忆——你们中有人必须去唱圣歌吗？或者你们在……唱过吗？

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:32.570
<v Woman>I am also super Jewish. I'm Israeli Jewish. So the level of--
<v Woman>我也是超级犹太人。我是以色列裔犹太人。所以那种……程度……

00:10:32.570 --> 00:10:33.850
<v Ira Glass>Oh, you totally out-Jew me.
<v Ira Glass>噢，你完全比我更像犹太人。

00:10:33.850 --> 00:11:01.630
<v Woman>Yeah. But I was in the Chicago Children's Choir. And it was a little kids choir. And the big thing that you do at Christmas is sing Christmas carols. So two things that I remember very well from Christmas choir was one was I got a solo that I got to do. Yeah. And I screwed up, and that was horrifying.
<v Woman>对。但我曾在芝加哥儿童合唱团。那是一个小孩子合唱团。在圣诞节你做的大事就是唱圣诞颂歌。关于圣诞合唱团，有两件事我记忆犹新，一是我有一次获得了独唱机会。是的。但我搞砸了，那真是太可怕了。

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:30.090
<v Woman>The other thing that just occurred to me now is-- so it was on the South Side of Chicago. So it was pretty racially diverse, meaning I usually was the minority, not just as a Jew but as a white person, in choir. And so we also did a lot of gospels, which is where I discovered that I can't clap. And it became something that I would practice at home.
<v Woman>另一件我现在突然想起的事是——那是在芝加哥南区。所以种族背景相当多样，意味着我通常是少数派，不仅作为犹太人，而且作为合唱团里的白人。所以我们也会唱很多福音歌曲，在那我发现我根本不会拍手。这成了我回家练习的事情。

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:34.570
<v Ira Glass>Could I just have clarification, just as a fact-checking matter? You would practice clapping.
<v Ira Glass>我能确认一下吗，作为事实核查？你回家练习拍手？

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:36.020
<v Woman>Yeah.
<v Woman>对。

00:11:36.020 --> 00:11:38.630
<v Ira Glass>To what? Like what would be the sound--
<v Ira Glass>对着什么？声音大概是——

00:11:38.630 --> 00:11:51.970
<v Woman>Just radio. I would just turn on the radio, and I'd be like, [CLAPPING] I would just, like, try to clap on the beat. Because if you don't know how to clap and you're in a choir clapping, it's terrifying.
<v Woman>就是收音机。我打开收音机，然后我会像这样，[拍手声]，我会尝试跟着节拍拍手。因为如果你不会拍手，又在合唱团里，那真的很可怕。

00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:54.640
<v Man>Bernice, I was watching you sing in the chorus.
<v Man>伯尼斯（Bernice），我刚才看着你在合唱团唱歌。

00:11:54.640 --> 00:12:01.600
<v Bernice>Oh, wow. You saw me? Wow.
<v Bernice>噢，哇。你看到我了？哇。

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:05.750
<v Man>I see everything. I'm Jesus.
<v Man>我看到一切。我是耶稣。

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:09.360
<v Bernice>Oh, wow-- this is-- this is so cool for me. This is huge.
<v Bernice>噢，哇——这——这对我来说太酷了。这太重要了。

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:11.100
<v Jesus>Oh, listen, it's an honor to meet you.
<v Jesus>噢，听着，见到你真是荣幸。

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:17.430
<v Bernice>Oh, my god. Thank you. Thank you, Jesus.
<v Bernice>噢，天哪。谢谢。谢谢你，耶稣。

00:12:17.430 --> 00:12:23.780
<v Jesus>You know I hear that all day? But it still means a lot when you say it.
<v Jesus>你知道我整天都能听到这些吗？但当你这么说时，意义依然重大。

00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:27.920
<v Bernice>Aw, Jesus, you're so nice.
<v Bernice>噢，耶稣，你真好。

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:34.580
<v Jesus>I got to tell you. You really should not be singing.
<v Jesus>我得告诉你。你真的不应该唱歌。

00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:38.470
<v Jesus>[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]
<v Jesus>[观众笑声]

00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:47.080
<v Bernice>[SINGING OFF-KEY] What do you mean? What do you mean by what you're saying?
<v Bernice>[跑调歌唱] 什么意思？你刚才说的话是什么意思？

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:47.910
<v Jesus>Well, I think--
<v Jesus>嗯，我想——

00:12:47.910 --> 00:12:50.600
<v Bernice>What do you mean?
<v Bernice>什么意思？

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:51.600
<v Jesus>Bernice, I--
<v Jesus>伯尼斯，我——

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:56.120
<v Bernice>Jesus, don't say that to me!
<v Bernice>耶稣，别那样对我说话！

00:12:56.120 --> 00:13:00.040
<v Jesus>Bernice, I think you're answering your own question there.
<v Jesus>伯尼斯，我觉得你自己在那回答了自己的问题。

00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:05.790
<v Holy Spirit>You know, I'm the Holy Spirit. And I just wanted to chime in. I think Bernice has a really nice voice. I--
<v Holy Spirit>你知道，我是圣灵。我只是想插一句。我觉得伯尼斯的声音真的很不错。我——

00:13:05.790 --> 00:13:09.280
<v Jesus>Dave, I told you not to come.
<v Jesus>戴夫，我告诉过你别来。

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:11.220
<v Holy Spirit>All right.
<v Holy Spirit>好吧。

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:17.900
<v Bernice>Well, maybe it's because I'm singing, like, gorgeous God songs. Maybe you want more of like a pop hit or something?
<v Bernice>嗯，也许是因为我唱的是那些华丽的赞美诗。也许你想要更多流行歌曲之类的？

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:33.460
<v Jesus>No, what I'm saying is that you are singing from your heart, but your heart is not good enough. It really puts other people off, and it's pushing people away from me. I need people. I need more people.
<v Jesus>不，我的意思是，你是用真心在唱，但你的真心还不够好。它确实让别人感到反感，并且在把我的人往外推。我需要人。我需要更多人。

00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:35.370
<v Jesus>[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]
<v Jesus>[观众笑声]

00:13:35.370 --> 00:13:49.300
<v Executive>We cut to a television executive's office. I got to say, we got this show, and the judges, they're too soft. And I think you're the exact kind of hard edge we need, Jesus.
<v Executive>我们切换到电视台高管办公室。我得说，我们要办这个节目，但评委们太软弱了。我觉得你正是我们需要的那种硬派人物，耶稣。

00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:58.350
<v Jesus>Well, I appreciate the compliment. But I would be happy to do the job, but let me also warn you that when I bring judgment, that's the end of the world.
<v Jesus>好吧，我感谢你的恭维。我很乐意做这份工作，但我也得警告你，当我降下审判时，那就是世界末日了。

00:13:58.350 --> 00:14:01.680
<v Executive>Listen, that's the kind of ratings bonanza I've been looking for, OK?
<v Executive>听着，那正是我一直在寻找的收视神话，明白吗？

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:02.130
<v Jesus>I'm in.
<v Jesus>我加入。

00:14:02.130 --> 00:14:12.820
<v Executive>CeeLo, Aguilera, they softball it, Adam Levine. They all feel bad. Nobody gives an honest opinion. I need somebody who's going to be harsh. I need somebody who's going to be brutal, who's going to say, "This is exactly how I see it."
<v Executive>希洛（CeeLo）、阿奎莱拉（Aguilera）、亚当·列维（Adam Levine），他们都太温和了。他们都觉得不好意思。没人给出真诚的意见。我需要一个会说狠话的人。我需要一个会冷酷无情的人，一个会说：“这就是我眼中的真相”的人。

00:14:12.820 --> 00:14:15.530
<v Jesus>Don't talk to me about Adam Levine, man.
<v Jesus>别跟我提亚当·列维，伙计。

00:14:15.530 --> 00:14:23.760
<v Executive>What do I gotta do to get you on my network? I know money means nothing to you. You're omnipotent. You know everything. You have access to everything. What do I gotta do to get you on my airwaves?
<v Executive>我要怎么做才能让你加入我的频道？我知道钱对你毫无意义。你是万能的。你知道一切。你拥有通往一切的门路。我要怎么做才能让你在我的电波里出现？

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:26.040
<v Jesus>I want you to live your life as I would live mine.
<v Jesus>我希望你按照我生活的方式去生活。

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:27.960
<v Executive>[GROAN]
<v Executive>[呻吟声]

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:30.700
<v Jesus>Scene!
<v Jesus>场景结束！

00:14:30.700 --> 00:14:36.590
<v Abraham>My Jewish brothers, welcome. I call this meeting.
<v Abraham>我的犹太兄弟们，欢迎。我召集这次会议。

00:14:36.590 --> 00:14:39.430
<v Jacob>You called this minion.
<v Jacob>你召集了这个……（单词误用）。

00:14:39.430 --> 00:14:40.120
<v Abraham>Thank you for--
<v Abraham>谢谢你——

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:42.120
<v Jacob>There's 10 of us. I got to say there's 10 of us.
<v Jacob>我们有10个人。我得说我们有10个人。

00:14:42.120 --> 00:15:04.400
<v Abraham>Thank you for the correction, Jacob. I call this minion to discuss our grievances with Christmas as a holiday. I know it's not our holiday. But I know that I have some concerns.
<v Abraham>谢谢你的纠正，雅各布。我召集这次会议是为了讨论我们对圣诞节作为假期的不满。我知道这不是我们的节日。但我知道我有一些担忧。

00:15:04.400 --> 00:15:09.940
<v Jacob>Brother Abraham, I think you should adjust your level of Jew-y. It's a little high.
<v Jacob>亚伯拉罕兄弟，我觉得你应该调整一下你的“犹太感”。有点太浓了。

00:15:09.940 --> 00:15:19.100
<v Abraham>Oh! Here it is. It's on my anklet here. There we are.
<v Abraham>噢！在这里。就在我的脚踝饰带上。找到了。

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:21.550
<v Jacob>Oh, well--
<v Jacob>噢，好吧——

00:15:21.550 --> 00:15:28.020
<v Comedian>Well, I don't like it!
<v Comedian>嗯，我不喜欢它！

00:15:28.020 --> 00:15:58.480
<v Ira Glass>Okay, this is Ira in the studio. I'm just going to stop the tape for a second just to make perfectly clear what's happening on stage right now. OK, so the first comedian in the scene, Mike Birbiglia, he had some trouble with his Jewish accent, as you heard, and he was teased about it. And then another comedian, Aidy Bryant, entered the scene. And she has so much trouble coming up with a Jewish accent that at some point, that just becomes what the scene is about, is her discomfort at this awful accent, and the other performers kind of egging her on and also ribbing her about it very affectionately. OK, back to the scene.
<v Ira Glass>好的，这是在录音室的艾拉。我暂停录音片刻，明确一下现在舞台上发生了什么。好的，第一个即兴表演者迈克·比比利亚，正如你们听到的，他在尝试犹太口音时遇到了一些困难，并被取笑了。然后另一个演员艾迪·布莱恩特加入了场景。她对于模仿犹太口音极其困难，以至于到最后，这直接变成了场景的核心——关于她对这种糟糕口音的不适，以及其他演员以一种极其亲昵的方式在戏弄和怂恿她。好了，回到场景。

00:15:58.480 --> 00:15:59.120
<v Jacob>Well--
<v Jacob>嗯——

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:11.310
<v Aidy Bryant>Well, I don't like it! I don't like the holiday one bit.
<v Aidy Bryant>嗯，我不喜欢它！我不喜欢这个节日一点点。

00:16:11.310 --> 00:16:15.450
<v Abraham>I want to welcome our brother from the Italian branch of Judaism.
<v Abraham>我想欢迎我们来自意大利犹太教分支的兄弟。

00:16:15.450 --> 00:16:21.960
<v Abraham>[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]
<v Abraham>[观众笑声]

