<v Stephanie Foo>[DUBSTEP MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Stephanie Foo>[Dubstep音乐播放]

00:45:21.430 --> 00:45:28.900
<v William Shatner>(SINGING) I want a deep-fried turkey. I want a moister, tastier turkey. I want a deep-fried turkey.
<v William Shatner>（歌唱）我想要深炸火鸡。我想要一只更湿润、更美味的火鸡。我想要深炸火鸡。

00:45:28.900 --> 00:45:32.030
<v Stephanie Foo>Amazingly, this was also released by State Farm.
<v Stephanie Foo>令人惊讶的是，这也是由State Farm发布的。

00:45:32.030 --> 00:45:45.120
<v William Shatner>(SINGING) There's a hunger. I was young and arrogant. Blinded by my hunger, I ignored safety. I want a deep-fried turkey. I want a moister, tastier, turkey.
<v William Shatner>（歌唱）有一种饥饿。我那时年轻又傲慢。被饥饿蒙蔽，我无视了安全。我想要深炸火鸡。我想要一只更湿润、更美味的火鸡。

00:45:45.120 --> 00:45:46.720
<v Stephanie Foo>Then there are the jackass videos--
<v Stephanie Foo>还有那些愚蠢的视频——

00:45:46.720 --> 00:45:47.180
<v Stephanie Foo>[OIL SIZZLING]
<v Stephanie Foo>[油滋滋声]

00:45:47.180 --> 00:45:47.640
<v Woman>Whoa!
<v Woman>哇！

00:45:47.640 --> 00:45:49.480
<v Man>Oh, my god.
<v Man>噢，天哪。

00:45:49.480 --> 00:46:16.940
<v Stephanie Foo>--where regular people, without safety gear, lower turkeys into deep fryers, sometimes from alarming heights, underneath trees and basketball hoops. Of course, there's a sub-genre here of people who actually wind up getting their decks on fire and fly into a panic. Just saying-- your average turkey fire incident injures four people-- four-- because it's a spectator sport, which is why fire departments do these videos every year.
<v Stephanie Foo>——普通人，没有防护装备，把火鸡放入油炸锅，有时从惊人的高度，在树下和篮球架下。当然，这里有个分支种类是人们最终烧毁了甲板并陷入恐慌。我只是说——你平均每次火鸡火灾事故会炸伤四个人——四个人——因为这是一种观赏性运动，这就是为什么消防部门每年都做这些视频。

00:46:16.940 --> 00:46:40.740
<v Stephanie Foo>But after my seventh time watching "Why You Should Never Deep Fry a Turkey," it occurred to me that there's another reason why they do them. I noticed one of the firefighters standing in the back. He was barely suppressing a grin. I zoomed in on him, watched it again-- definitely a grin. So I called him to ask about it. His name is Lieutenant Daniel Akerman of the Escambia County Fire Rescue.
<v Stephanie Foo>但在我第七次观看“为什么你永远不应该深炸火鸡”之后，我想到他们这样做的另一个原因。我注意到其中一名站在后面的消防员。他几乎掩盖不住他的咧嘴笑。我放大看了看他，又看了一遍——绝对是咧嘴笑。所以我打电话问他这件事。他的名字是埃斯坎比亚县消防救援队的丹尼尔·阿克曼（Daniel Akerman）中尉。

00:46:40.740 --> 00:46:49.290
<v Daniel Akerman>Definitely one of the highlights of being in the office. When you actually drop that turkey, you're not 100% sure how it's going to come out.
<v Daniel Akerman>绝对是在办公室里最棒的时刻之一。当你真的放下那只火鸡，你并不百分之百确定它会发生什么。

00:46:49.290 --> 00:46:53.810
<v Stephanie Foo>Here's some of the guys from the Fargo Fire Department. They have one of my favorite videos.
<v Stephanie Foo>这是来自法戈（Fargo）消防局的一些伙计。他们有我最喜欢的视频之一。

00:46:53.810 --> 00:46:54.240
<v Stephanie Foo>Hi.
<v Stephanie Foo>嗨。

00:46:54.240 --> 00:46:55.540
<v Man>Hello.
<v Man>你好。

00:46:55.540 --> 00:46:55.970
<v Man>Hi.
<v Man>嗨。

00:46:55.970 --> 00:46:59.220
<v Stephanie Foo>Do all of you guys like setting turkeys on fire?
<v Stephanie Foo>你们所有人喜欢点燃火鸡吗？

00:46:59.220 --> 00:47:01.020
<v Stephanie Foo>[LAUGHTER]
<v Stephanie Foo>[笑声]

00:47:01.020 --> 00:47:06.120
<v Man>Yeah, you're doing the opposite of what you're supposed to be doing. You're supposed to be putting out fire, but you get to start one.
<v Man>对，你们在做你们本该做的相反的事。你们本该灭火，但你们却能点燃一团。

00:47:06.120 --> 00:47:06.980
<v Man>Yeah, it's fun.
<v Man>对，这很好玩。

00:47:06.980 --> 00:47:11.150
<v Stephanie Foo>What's your favorite part about the flaming turkey tradition?
<v Stephanie Foo>关于火鸡着火的传统，你最喜欢的部分是什么？

00:47:11.150 --> 00:47:13.570
<v Man>Making as big a fireball as you can.
<v Man>尽可能制造一个巨大的火球。

00:47:13.570 --> 00:47:14.490
<v Man>Oh, yeah.
<v Man>噢，对。

00:47:14.490 --> 00:47:19.230
<v Stephanie Foo>And they're good at it. Fargo's fireball is two stories tall.
<v Stephanie Foo>他们很擅长。法戈的火球有两层楼高。

00:47:19.230 --> 00:47:22.140
<v Stephanie Foo>[FIRE HISSING]
<v Stephanie Foo>[火的嘶嘶声]

00:47:22.140 --> 00:47:25.050
<v Stephanie Foo>Your explosion is huge.
<v Stephanie Foo>你们的爆炸太大了。

00:47:25.050 --> 00:47:25.520
<v Man>Yeah.
<v Man>对。

00:47:25.520 --> 00:47:29.860
<v Stephanie Foo>What is happening where you are able to get it to be so big?
<v Stephanie Foo>到底发生了什么，让你们能弄得这么大？

00:47:29.860 --> 00:47:40.730
<v Man>Sure. Well, we're not following any of the safety recommendations to get it to be that dramatic to show people absolute worst case what can happen.
<v Man>当然。嗯，为了展示最糟糕的情况，我们没遵循任何安全建议来让它变得那么具有戏剧性。

00:47:40.730 --> 00:47:42.880
<v Stephanie Foo>Did you guys practice?
<v Stephanie Foo>你们练习过吗？

00:47:42.880 --> 00:47:54.430
<v Man>Yeah. We did find out through practice that the more oil we put in the pot, by filling it to the top, made the fireball as big as possible.
<v Man>对。我们通过练习确实发现，通过把油加满，直到锅顶，能让火球尽可能大。

00:47:54.430 --> 00:48:15.790
<v Stephanie Foo>Quick science lesson. These frozen turkeys are filled with ice. Ice, of course, is water. And water does not mix with oil. So when the turkeys hit the oil, that ice turns to water turns to steam, which pushes the oil out of the pot, and when that hits the flame underneath, boom.
<v Stephanie Foo>科学小课堂。这些冷冻火鸡里充满了冰。冰，当然，是水。而水不和油混合。所以当火鸡撞击油时，冰变成水变成蒸汽，它把油从锅里推出来，当蒸汽接触到底部的火焰时，砰。

00:48:15.790 --> 00:48:48.530
<v Stephanie Foo>So your basic turkey frying don'ts are as follows. One, don't put in a frozen turkey. You should thaw your turkey 24 hours for every five pounds. That's three whole days for a 15-pounder. Two, don't put in a wet turkey or a too-big turkey. Three, don't overfill the pot with oil or overheat the oil. Four, do not do this inside. And lastly, most fire departments would tell you the most important don't is just please, don't deep fry a turkey at all.
<v Stephanie Foo>所以你基本的炸火鸡禁忌如下。第一，不要放入冷冻火鸡。你应该每五磅解冻火鸡24小时。15磅的火鸡需要整整三天。第二，不要放入湿火鸡或太大的火鸡。第三，不要把油加得太满或过度加热油。第四，不要在室内做这个。最后，大多数消防部门会告诉你最重要的禁忌就是求你，根本不要去深炸火鸡。

00:48:48.530 --> 00:49:11.870
<v Stephanie Foo>Fargo double did all the don'ts. Like, while some firefighters heat their demonstration pots to 350, Fargo took theirs to 700. The pot of oil is obviously already in flames and overflowing before the turkey gets anywhere near it, like an active volcano.
<v Stephanie Foo>法戈（Fargo）把所有的禁忌都做了。比如，虽然一些消防员将演示锅加热到350度，法戈把它们加热到了700度。油锅在火鸡接近它之前显然就已经着火并溢出了，就像一座活火山。

00:49:11.870 --> 00:49:32.880
<v Stephanie Foo>I managed to find the first turkey fire video from 2002. It's called "Turkey Fryer-- Your Dinner for Your Life?" It's hosted by this earnest guy in a hardhat named John Drengenberg, who was not in it for the fun, the fame, or the fireballs. I asked him, is he proud of being the originator of this new holiday tradition, the turkey explosion video?
<v Stephanie Foo>我设法找到了2002年的第一个火鸡火灾视频。它叫“油炸锅——你的晚餐还是你的命？”它由一个戴安全帽、名叫约翰·德伦根伯格（John Drengenberg）的真诚家伙主持，他不是为了好玩、出名或火球才做这个的。我问他，作为这个新节日传统——火鸡爆炸视频的鼻祖，他感到自豪吗？

00:49:32.880 --> 00:49:36.550
<v John Drengenberg>OK, first of all, I'm an engineer. So they aren't explosions.
<v John Drengenberg>好的，首先，我是个工程师。所以它们不是爆炸。

00:49:36.550 --> 00:49:37.570
<v Stephanie Foo>Oh, OK. I'm sorry.
<v Stephanie Foo>噢，好的。抱歉。

00:49:37.570 --> 00:49:38.050
<v John Drengenberg>OK.
<v John Drengenberg>好的。

00:49:38.050 --> 00:49:41.850
<v Stephanie Foo>So what would the appropriate terminology be?
<v Stephanie Foo>那合适的术语应该是什么？

00:49:41.850 --> 00:49:46.950
<v John Drengenberg>Flaming oil is being expelled from the turkey fryer.
<v John Drengenberg>燃烧的油正在从火鸡油炸锅中喷出。

00:49:46.950 --> 00:49:53.790
<v Stephanie Foo>So how do you feel about being the originator of the tradition of flaming turkey oil videos?
<v Stephanie Foo>所以，作为燃烧火鸡油视频传统的鼻祖，你感觉如何？

00:49:53.790 --> 00:49:59.640
<v John Drengenberg>It's not unusual. It's in keeping with our mission of alerting people to safety issues.
<v John Drengenberg>这不罕见。这符合我们提醒人们注意安全问题的使命。

00:49:59.640 --> 00:50:04.870
<v Stephanie Foo>Sure. But you know, outside of the mission, what does it make you feel?
<v Stephanie Foo>当然。但你知道，在使命之外，它让你感觉到了什么？

00:50:04.870 --> 00:50:12.370
<v John Drengenberg>Like we've accomplished a goal of alerting people to the dangers.
<v John Drengenberg>就像我们已经完成了提醒人们注意危险的目标。

00:50:12.370 --> 00:50:52.300
<v Stephanie Foo>John works for Underwriters Laboratories. They test products to make sure they're safe. I just walked over to the part of our office that has a toaster and a coffee grinder, and I found UL logos on everything, even the light bulbs. And so when John started testing turkey deep fryers at UL in 2002, he found them so egregiously dangerous and prone to failure that they put out a press release calling for stronger turkey fryer guidelines. And because most people don't read press releases, he figured they had to make a PSA informing people about those dangers, too, because John sees danger around us all the time. And he does not think the turkey videos are sensationalistic in the slightest.
<v Stephanie Foo>约翰为Underwriters Laboratories（UL）工作。他们测试产品以确保它们安全。我刚才走到我们办公室有烤面包机和咖啡研磨机的地方，我发现UL标志在一切上，甚至灯泡上。所以当约翰在2002年在UL开始测试深炸锅时，他发现它们非常危险且容易失效，于是他们发布了一份新闻稿呼吁加强炸火鸡准则。因为大多数人不读新闻稿，他认为他们必须制作一个公共服务广告告知人们那些危险，因为约翰随时随地都能看到危险。他一点也不认为火鸡视频是耸人听闻的。

00:50:52.300 --> 00:51:09.480
<v John Drengenberg>I think we tried to show the reality. Even if you're using it correctly, and a dog runs by it or it could bump into the stand and knock the turkey fryer down, which would cause some very serious burns on a pet, as well as a child.
<v John Drengenberg>我想我们尝试展示的是现实。即使你正确使用它，如果有一条狗跑过去，或者它撞到了架子并把火鸡炸锅弄倒了，那会导致宠物以及孩子严重烧伤。

00:51:09.480 --> 00:51:17.730
<v Stephanie Foo>Do your friends and family ever-- are you, like, calling them out and being like, please don't do that. And are they like, oh, come on. Just chill out about it. It's fine.
<v Stephanie Foo>你的朋友和家人有没有——你有没有叫住他们，说，求求你们别那样做。而他们说，噢，拜托。冷静点。没事的。

00:51:17.730 --> 00:51:44.310
<v John Drengenberg>Well, the fact is they call me Mr. Safety. And I've actually cut a cord off my mother-in-law's fan, because it was so-- the cord was all ratty. And you know, it was cracked. The insulation was cracked, and there was bare wire on it. And I said, you can't plug this fan in. And she said, well, I need it. It keeps me cool. And I said, it's not good for you. So I unplugged it myself and I cut the cord off so she--
<v John Drengenberg>嗯，事实是他们叫我“安全先生”。我甚至切断了我岳母风扇的电线，因为它太——电线很破旧。你知道，它裂开了。绝缘层裂开了，裸露着金属线。我说，你不能插这个风扇。她说，我需要它。它让我凉快。我说，这不安全。所以我自己拔掉它并切断了电线，所以她——

00:51:44.310 --> 00:51:47.480
<v Stephanie Foo>Why didn't you just take the fan?
<v Stephanie Foo>为什么你不直接拿走风扇？

00:51:47.480 --> 00:51:49.380
<v John Drengenberg>Well, I could have done that.
<v John Drengenberg>嗯，我本来可以那么做的。

00:51:49.380 --> 00:51:50.800
<v Stephanie Foo>So you're a bit of a worrier.
<v Stephanie Foo>所以你是个爱操心的人。

00:51:50.800 --> 00:52:00.640
<v John Drengenberg>Well, I've got a bit of experience in the field of product safety.
<v John Drengenberg>嗯，我在产品安全领域有一点经验。

00:52:00.640 --> 00:52:07.270
<v Stephanie Foo>Do you have any other really important holiday cautionary tips for us before I let you go?
<v Stephanie Foo>在让你走之前，你还有什么对我们很重要的节日提醒吗？

00:52:07.270 --> 00:52:32.220
<v John Drengenberg>Make sure your smoke alarm is working. Make sure you water your tree. You have to run your hand along on  the branches in the tree lot to make sure you don't end up with a handful of needles. If you do, the tree is too dry. You should never bring it home. There are statistics you can get, by the way, Stephanie, from places like the Consumer Product Safety Commission on just how many people are injured each year while decorating. I mean, it's like 18,000 people hurt themselves decorating.
<v John Drengenberg>确保你的烟雾报警器工作正常。确保给树浇水。你必须在树场沿着树枝滑动手，以确保你最终不会只抓到一把针叶。如果那样，树太干了。你永远不该带它回家。顺便说一句，斯蒂芬妮，你可以从像消费品安全委员会这样的地方获取数据，关于每年有多少人在装饰时受伤。我是说，大概有18000人在装饰时弄伤了自己。

00:52:32.220 --> 00:52:33.670
<v Stephanie Foo>Oh, my god.
<v Stephanie Foo>噢，天哪。

00:52:33.670 --> 00:52:37.960
<v John Drengenberg>Yeah, we could talk for two hours, if you'd like, about cautionary tips.
<v John Drengenberg>对，如果你愿意，我们可以聊两个小时关于预防措施。

00:52:37.960 --> 00:52:41.050
<v Stephanie Foo>Jeez, well, why even celebrate at all?
<v Stephanie Foo>天呐，那为什么还要过节？

00:52:41.050 --> 00:52:55.920
<v John Drengenberg>Oh, that's-- we're not grinches here. We just want to keep people safe. That's our mission, whatever your holiday. And it's not just Christmas. It's Hanukkah. It's Kwanzaa, all of that. And all of those holidays, by the way, use candles.
<v John Drengenberg>噢，那——我们不是格林奇（圣诞怪杰）。我们只是想保护人们的安全。那是我们的使命，无论你过什么节日。而且不仅仅是圣诞节。它是光明节。它是宽扎节（Kwanzaa），所有的这些。顺便说一句，所有这些节日都使用蜡烛。

00:52:55.920 --> 00:52:57.320
<v Stephanie Foo>[LAUGHS]
<v Stephanie Foo>[笑声]

00:52:57.320 --> 00:53:03.600
<v John Drengenberg>You want go into candles? You ready for candles? Can you handle it?
<v John Drengenberg>你想聊聊蜡烛吗？准备好聊蜡烛了吗？你能应付吗？

00:53:03.600 --> 00:53:24.630
<v Stephanie Foo>John suggests that in lieu of candles this year, try using LED tealights instead. And if you think that's too drastic, please go to YouTube right now and type in the words "Christmas tree fires." And then help John have a Merry Christmas. Go water your tree.
<v Stephanie Foo>约翰建议今年用LED茶蜡代替蜡烛。如果你觉得那太极端，请现在去YouTube输入单词“圣诞树火灾”。然后帮约翰过个圣诞快乐。去浇树吧。

00:53:24.630 --> 00:53:51.860
<v Ira Glass>Stephanie Foo is one of the producers of our program. We checked, by the way, with the Consumer Product Safety Commission and learned that on average, there were only about 13-- 13-- turkey fryer-related incidents a year, which means one of two things, OK? Either number one, the hundreds of turkey fryer fire videos online are unnecessary. Or, number two, the hundreds of turkey fryer fire videos online are working.
<v Ira Glass>斯蒂芬妮·富是我们节目的制片人之一。顺便说一句，我们和消费品安全委员会核实后得知，平均每年只有大约13起——13起——与油炸锅相关的事故，这意味着两件事之一，好吗？要么第一，网上的数百个油炸锅火灾视频是不必要的。要么第二，网上的数百个油炸锅火灾视频是有效的。

00:53:51.860 --> 00:54:12.940
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, "The First Noelle." Andre is six years old. And this is his first Christmas with, you know, Santa and presents and the whole deal. And already, it's been like one incredible thing after another. Till recently, he and his little brother, Luke, who's four, had been in an orphanage in the Democratic Republic of Congo. And in the orphanage, Christmas was not much of a thing.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，“第一首Noelle”。安德烈（Andre）六岁。这是他第一个圣诞节，你知道，有圣诞老人、礼物和全套东西。而且这已经是接连不断的神奇体验了。直到最近，他和弟弟卢克（Luke）四岁，还住在刚果民主共和国的一家孤儿院。在孤儿院，圣诞节并不算什么。

00:54:12.940 --> 00:54:19.020
<v Ira Glass>But now they're living outside Seattle, where it definitely is a thing. Weeks ago, Christmas lights started popping up.
<v Ira Glass>但现在他们住在西雅图郊外，那里圣诞节意义重大。几周前，圣诞灯饰开始出现了。

00:54:19.020 --> 00:54:27.260
<v Jennefer>And they would point to that and say, Mom, Mom, look at that. And so we started to explain, yeah, that's for Christmas. It's to celebrate Christmas.
<v Jennefer>他们会指着灯说，妈妈，妈妈，看那个。所以我们就开始解释，对，那是为圣诞节。那是为了庆祝圣诞节。

00:54:27.260 --> 00:54:30.510
<v Ira Glass>This is Jennefer. She and her husband adopted Andre and Luke.
<v Ira Glass>这是詹妮弗（Jennefer）。她和丈夫收养了安德烈和卢克。

00:54:30.510 --> 00:54:43.510
<v Jennefer>And then we drove through the neighborhood, just with the purpose of looking for lights, they were just non-stop. It didn't matter how big the light display was. They were amazed.
<v Jennefer>然后我们开车穿过街区，专门为了看灯，他们简直停不下来。灯饰多大都不重要。他们太惊讶了。

00:54:43.510 --> 00:54:45.430
<v Child>Wow! Wow!
<v Child>哇！哇！

00:54:45.430 --> 00:54:47.820
<v Child>But look at Snoopy!
<v Child>但看史努比（Snoopy）！

00:54:47.820 --> 00:54:48.780
<v Child>Bye!
<v Child>再见！

00:54:48.780 --> 00:54:49.260
<v Child>Snoopy!
<v Child>史努比！

00:54:49.260 --> 00:54:49.740
<v Child>[LAUGHTER]
<v Child>[笑声]

00:54:49.740 --> 00:54:56.820
<v Ira Glass>This is obviously from a recording they made on that drive. Jennefer and her husband also have two little girls, seven and five, who you can hear on the recording.
<v Ira Glass>这显然是他们在那次车程中录下的录音。詹妮弗和丈夫还有两个小女孩，七岁和五岁，你可以在录音中听到她们的声音。

00:54:56.820 --> 00:54:57.770
<v Ira Glass>[KIDS OOHING AND AHHING]
<v Ira Glass>[孩子们的惊叹声]

00:54:57.770 --> 00:55:01.100
<v Man>Do you see two of them in the [INAUDIBLE]?
<v Man>你们能看到[听不清]里的那两个吗？

00:55:01.100 --> 00:55:02.540
<v Child>Whoa.
<v Child>哇。

00:55:02.540 --> 00:55:08.610
<v Jennefer>And no one wanted to go home. They just couldn't get enough of it.
<v Jennefer>而且没人想回家。他们就是看不够。

00:55:08.610 --> 00:55:09.460
<v Child>Whoa.
<v Child>哇。

00:55:09.460 --> 00:55:34.890
<v Ira Glass>It's been kind of a crash course since the boys arrived in the States. They just got here this November. They'd heard some Christmas basics before. This adoption process has taken three years. And Jennefer lived with the boys in Kinshasa for most of this year, and she talked about Christmas a little bit with the boys. But you know, if you think about it, it is a lot to cover. With her girls, you know, from the time that they could speak, they were basically living in a world with Christmas and Santa and all that, but with the boys--
<v Ira Glass>自从男孩们来到美国，这简直是一场速成课程。他们今年11月才到。他们以前听说过一些圣诞基础知识。这个收养过程花了三年。詹妮弗今年大部分时间都和男孩们住在金沙萨，她和男孩们谈了一些关于圣诞节的事。但你知道，如果你想想看，要涵盖的内容实在太多了。对于她的女孩，你知道，从她们会说话开始，她们基本上就生活在有圣诞节、圣诞老人等等的世界里，但对于这些男孩——

00:55:34.890 --> 00:55:46.640
<v Ira Glass>It must have been strange for you. You really have to start at the beginning and walk through a whole lot of stuff. And it's like, there's reindeer, and there's like this whole-- it's really like kind of a lot to explain, right?
<v Ira Glass>这对你来说一定很奇怪。你真的得从零开始，走过一大堆东西。就像，有驯鹿，有这个整个——解释起来真的很多，对吧？

00:55:46.640 --> 00:56:28.330
<v Jennefer>Right, yeah. We had to hit a lot of basics. Who is Rudolph? Who is even Santa? I mean, the fact that we had a Christmas tree in our house, oh. And even that we went to pick out a Christmas tree and then tied this tree onto our car-- you know, the little things that we just do every year so we don't really think about it as strange. But when you were a kid and you see a tree tied to a car, or you're tying it to your own car, he'd be like, "Why? What are they doing to the tree? Why are they putting it on the roof?" And we opened up the sunroof while he was in there, just the window part so he could see it. And he just stared at it in awe.
<v Jennefer>对，是的。我们不得不抓基础。鲁道夫是谁？圣诞老人又是谁？我是说，我们在房子里有棵圣诞树，噢。甚至我们去挑选圣诞树，然后把树绑在我们的车上——你知道，那些我们每年都做所以并不觉得奇怪的小事。但当你还是个孩子，看到一棵树绑在车上，或者你把它绑在自己的车上时，他会想，“为什么？他们在对树做什么？为什么他们要把它放在车顶？”他在车里时我们打开了天窗，只是窗户那部分，好让他能看见。他只是敬畏地盯着它看。

00:56:28.330 --> 00:56:37.490
<v Ira Glass>One of the recordings you made is of, I guess, Andre saying over and over, "Are you kidding me, guys? Are you kidding me, guys? Are you kidding me, guys?" What's that about?
<v Ira Glass>你做的一段录音中，我猜是安德烈一遍又一遍地说，“你们在开玩笑吗，伙计们？你们在开玩笑吗，伙计们？你们在开玩笑吗，伙计们？”那是什么情况？

00:56:37.490 --> 00:56:54.840
<v Jennefer>Yeah, he kept opening new ornaments and seeing a new ornament. We have a tradition in our family that we hang up an ornament with a picture of each child each year. So they were both really excited to see their own faces on ornaments as they hung them up. So--
<v Jennefer>对，他不停地拆开新饰品，看到一个新饰品。我们家族有个传统，每年悬挂一个带有每个孩子照片的饰品。所以他们看到饰品挂上去时上面有自己的脸，都非常兴奋。所以——

00:56:54.840 --> 00:57:02.340
<v Andre>Are you kidding me, guys? Are you kidding me, guys? Are you kidding me, guys?
<v Andre>你们在开玩笑吗，伙计们？你们在开玩笑吗，伙计们？你们在开玩笑吗，伙计们？

00:57:02.340 --> 00:57:10.480
<v Jennefer>His English is so new, so he usually uses that when he sees something that he really likes.
<v Jennefer>他的英语太新了，所以他通常在看到自己真正喜欢的东西时会用那句话。

00:57:10.480 --> 00:57:18.430
<v Ira Glass>Growing up in the orphanage, not only did they not have ornaments and trees and Santa, they didn't get presents-- not for Christmas, not for birthdays.
<v Ira Glass>在孤儿院长大，他们不仅没有饰品、树和圣诞老人，他们甚至没有礼物——无论是圣诞节还是生日。

00:57:18.430 --> 00:57:25.120
<v Jennefer>Yeah, you know, they didn't really have belongings before. Everything was shared at the orphanage.
<v Jennefer>对，你知道，他们之前没有真正的个人物品。孤儿院里一切都是共享的。

00:57:25.120 --> 00:57:31.610
<v Ira Glass>Neither of them ever asked anybody for a gift or picked out a present to give to somebody. That's all new.
<v Ira Glass>他们中没人要求过别人给礼物，或者挑一份礼物给别人。那全是新鲜事。

00:57:31.610 --> 00:57:46.150
<v Ira Glass>Of course, Christmas is just one of the new things that they're experiencing in their new lives in this country. The orphanage that they lived in was on the outskirts of Kinshasa and didn't have electricity or running water. Even going to a store was a new experience for the boys when they joined Jennefer's family.
<v Ira Glass>当然，圣诞节只是他们在国家新生活中体验到的新鲜事物之一。他们居住的孤儿院在金沙萨郊外，没有电和自来水。甚至去商店对男孩们来说也是加入詹妮弗家庭后的全新体验。

00:57:46.150 --> 00:57:55.640
<v Jennefer>Everything's amazing right now, so it's not hard to believe that Santa comes and brings you gifts. That's just one of the many things.
<v Jennefer>现在一切都很神奇，所以不难相信圣诞老人来了并给你带礼物。那只是清单上众多事情之一。

00:57:55.640 --> 00:58:54.870
<v Ira Glass>Compared with the incredibleness of, you know, living in a house with water that comes out of faucets, and a play room, and a family, Santa Claus, the idea of Santa Claus, that's just really just like one more thing on the list, right? A guy flies in with a sleigh in the middle of the night with a bag of presents? Why not? It's all kind of amazing if we just let ourselves see it that way.
<v Ira Glass>相比于生活在有自来水龙头、游戏室和家庭的房子里那种不可思议，圣诞老人，圣诞老人的想法，那真的只是列表上的又一件事情，对吧？一个家伙半夜带着雪橇和一袋礼物飞来？为什么不呢？如果我们让自己那样看它，这一切都很神奇。

